  {"id":"1ff7db0e-6dcb-4d3a-86f7-198f5f992908","term":"Mixture stop","verdict":"MINOR","suggestions":[
    {"field":"definition","severity":"Low","issue":"The parenthetical '(mutation pitches)' is attached to 'octaves and fifths,' but in standard organ terminology only the non-octave ranks (fifth-sounding quints, and thirds) are mutations; octave-sounding ranks are NOT mutations. Web-verified via Wikipedia 'Mutation (organ stop)' and 'Mixture (organ stop)': a mutation sounds a pitch other than the unison or its octaves, i.e. a fifth or third. Applying 'mutation pitches' to the octave ranks mislabels them.","suggestion":"Reword to e.g. 'tuned to octaves and fifths above the unison (the fifth-sounding ranks being mutation pitches)' so the mutation label applies only to the non-octave ranks."}
